1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,087
This is the reconstruction of Advanced Tinkering's original magnetron, not our separate redesign.

2
00:00:06,087 --> 00:00:11,041
The complete study assembly contains one hundred and twenty solid component instances.

3
00:00:11,041 --> 00:00:15,015
We will build it up in functional groups and inspect the connections.

4
00:00:15,015 --> 00:00:18,471
The supplied machined shapes retain their original geometry.

5
00:00:18,471 --> 00:00:23,540
Missing fasteners and service components are reconstructed or marked as reference parts.

6
00:00:23,540 --> 00:00:29,300
The animation explains the CAD assembly; it is not a qualified manufacturing or operating procedure.

7
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:35,524
Here are the main layers, separated along their common axis for inspection.

8
00:00:35,524 --> 00:00:41,129
Starting at the rear, we have the bottom body, the main PTFE ring, and the intermediate spacer.

9
00:00:41,129 --> 00:00:46,261
Above them sit the steel return yoke, magnets, copper closure, and copper cooling body.

10
00:00:46,261 --> 00:00:51,334
The target and its retaining ring are at the front, followed by the dark space shield.

11
00:00:51,334 --> 00:00:57,233
The long outer tube and small hardware are hidden in this view so the layer relationships are clear.

12
00:00:59,033 --> 00:01:01,387
The two copper parts do different jobs.

13
00:01:01,387 --> 00:01:04,707
The cooling body supports the target on its front face.

14
00:01:04,707 --> 00:01:09,294
Its rear face contains the blind magnet wells and the annular water channel.

15
00:01:09,294 --> 00:01:13,760
The backplate closes that channel and carries the two coolant connections.

16
00:01:13,760 --> 00:01:17,925
For inspection, the backplate is turned over beside the cooling body.

17
00:01:17,925 --> 00:01:19,615
This is only a viewing pose.

18
00:01:19,615 --> 00:01:23,900
The matching faces return to their original orientation when assembled.

19
00:01:25,700 --> 00:01:28,966
Two O rings sit in the front face of the copper backplate.

20
00:01:28,966 --> 00:01:34,709
The fourteen point five by two millimetre inner ring isolates the water from the center magnet region.

21
00:01:34,709 --> 00:01:39,213
The thirty five by two millimetre outer ring isolates it from the outer magnets.

22
00:01:39,213 --> 00:01:46,533
The faces close onto the one point four five millimetre gland depth, giving twenty seven point five percent nominal axial squeeze.

23
00:01:46,533 --> 00:01:53,233
The displayed ring shapes illustrate the installed occupied volume; they are not a prediction of elastomer deformation.

24
00:01:55,033 --> 00:01:59,879
Twelve screws enter from the rear of the copper backplate and engage the cooling body.

25
00:01:59,879 --> 00:02:04,330
Their heads sit in the backplate counterbores, leaving room for the steel yoke.

26
00:02:04,330 --> 00:02:11,148
In this reconstruction they are represented as M three by sixteen socket head screws, selected to fit the measured stack.

27
00:02:11,148 --> 00:02:13,966
The actual author screw lengths were not supplied.

28
00:02:13,966 --> 00:02:18,924
These screws hold the two copper parts together; the two O rings provide the face seals.

29
00:02:18,924 --> 00:02:21,967
Changing a target does not require opening this joint.

30
00:02:23,767 --> 00:02:28,749
The original design uses twelve outer rod magnets on a fifty millimetre pitch circle.

31
00:02:28,749 --> 00:02:32,617
Each is eight millimetres in diameter and twenty millimetres long.

32
00:02:32,617 --> 00:02:35,958
Two twelve by ten millimetre discs form the center stack.

33
00:02:35,958 --> 00:02:39,768
The magnet pack enters the aligned dry bores in the copper parts.

34
00:02:39,768 --> 00:02:46,039
The center stack faces north toward the target and the outer rods face south, as shown in the source video.

35
00:02:46,039 --> 00:02:49,614
The tips stop half a millimetre short of the blind well ends.

36
00:02:49,614 --> 00:02:51,900
Magnetic forces are not simulated here.

37
00:02:53,700 --> 00:02:58,556
The mild steel yoke contacts the rear magnet faces and provides a magnetic return path.

38
00:02:58,556 --> 00:03:02,017
A separate retaining screw secures it to the copper backplate.

39
00:03:02,017 --> 00:03:10,054
The source model has a thread sized bore in both members at that screw, so the fastening detail remains flagged rather than silently redesigned.

40
00:03:10,054 --> 00:03:13,459
Two coolant fittings pass through the larger yoke clearances.

41
00:03:13,459 --> 00:03:18,148
The high voltage conductor is shown hooked under its separate copper terminal screw.

42
00:03:18,148 --> 00:03:22,167
Fitting identity and the exact wire shape are reference representations.

43
00:03:23,967 --> 00:03:27,934
The intermediate spacer surrounds the copper closure and magnet yoke.

44
00:03:27,934 --> 00:03:34,545
Before bringing it against the cooling body, the seventy six by two millimetre ring occupies its front face groove.

45
00:03:34,545 --> 00:03:37,535
This is a different seal from the two water O rings.

46
00:03:37,535 --> 00:03:41,157
It closes the dry interior against the vacuum facing periphery.

47
00:03:41,157 --> 00:03:45,699
The spacer advances around the yoke until its front face meets the copper body.

48
00:03:45,699 --> 00:03:49,033
The shared source coordinates define that fitted position.

49
00:03:50,833 --> 00:03:55,199
Twelve long screws pass through the spacer and engage the copper cooling body.

50
00:03:55,199 --> 00:03:57,829
Their heads are on the rear face of the spacer.

51
00:03:57,829 --> 00:04:03,258
The reconstructed M three by thirty five length provides engagement in the measured copper holes.

52
00:04:03,258 --> 00:04:06,952
These are not the twelve screws that later attach the bottom body.

53
00:04:06,952 --> 00:04:09,415
The two families use different bolt circles.

54
00:04:09,415 --> 00:04:14,900
Additional holes in the main PTFE ring provide clearance around these head attachment screw heads.

55
00:04:16,700 --> 00:04:21,467
A sixty two by two millimetre O ring seals the spacer to the main PTFE insulator.

56
00:04:21,467 --> 00:04:26,822
The stepped collar locates in the spacer opening, and the large ring face seats against it.

57
00:04:26,822 --> 00:04:34,649
This part has two sets of holes: clearance for the head attachment screw heads, and passages for the separate base attachment screws.

58
00:04:34,649 --> 00:04:38,945
The PTFE separates the live upper assembly from the grounded bottom body.

59
00:04:38,945 --> 00:04:43,300
Its geometric fit alone does not establish an electrical operating rating.

60
00:04:45,100 --> 00:04:49,158
The bottom body also needs insulation around its twelve attachment screws.

61
00:04:49,158 --> 00:04:54,093
Each shoulder sleeve lines a through hole and places a PTFE flange beneath the screw head.

62
00:04:54,093 --> 00:04:57,712
The main insulator ring does not replace these individual sleeves.

63
00:04:57,712 --> 00:05:03,854
Their reconstructed shape fits the measured five point eight millimetre bores and eight millimetre counterbores.

64
00:05:03,854 --> 00:05:11,367
The exact author sleeve drawing was not supplied, so the flange thickness and profile are identified as fitted reconstruction dimensions.

65
00:05:13,167 --> 00:05:18,356
The second sixty two by two millimetre ring sits in the top face of the grounded bottom body.

66
00:05:18,356 --> 00:05:22,039
The body and its prepared sleeves advance onto the main PTFE ring.

67
00:05:22,039 --> 00:05:27,842
Twelve separate base screws pass through the sleeves and insulator, then engage the intermediate spacer.

68
00:05:27,842 --> 00:05:32,696
Their heads remain electrically separated from the grounded body by the sleeve flanges.

69
00:05:32,696 --> 00:05:41,233
The source video describes arcing concerns around this region; reproducing the CAD geometry does not resolve those concerns or establish safe clearances.

70
00:05:43,033 --> 00:05:47,613
The central tube attaches beneath the bottom body using a KF sixteen style joint.

71
00:05:47,613 --> 00:05:54,116
The centering ring locates the seal, the tube flange approaches, and two semicircular clamp halves close around it.

72
00:05:54,116 --> 00:05:56,547
Six screws draw the clamp against the base.

73
00:05:56,547 --> 00:05:59,770
The source hole geometry supports M five sized fasteners.

74
00:05:59,770 --> 00:06:08,308
The flange and seal models are compatible reference shapes, and the displayed two hundred millimetre tube length is not an original measured dimension.

75
00:06:08,308 --> 00:06:13,567
The flexible services are threaded through this route before their connections are finalized.

76
00:06:15,367 --> 00:06:17,061
Now reveal the cooling space.

77
00:06:17,061 --> 00:06:23,197
Water comes through one hose and fitting into the copper annulus, then returns through the opposite port.

78
00:06:23,197 --> 00:06:28,573
Because the original passage is a continuous ring, water can travel around both semicircles.

79
00:06:28,573 --> 00:06:31,203
There is no C channel divider in this design.

80
00:06:31,203 --> 00:06:35,586
The inner and outer O rings separate the water from the dry magnet regions.

81
00:06:35,586 --> 00:06:43,767
Moving markers illustrate the possible routes; they do not represent a computed flow split, velocity, pressure loss, or cooling performance.

82
00:06:45,567 --> 00:06:48,497
There are three different regions to keep straight.

83
00:06:48,497 --> 00:06:52,461
Water is confined to the annular cooling circuit and its connections.

84
00:06:52,461 --> 00:06:57,287
The dry interior communicates with room atmosphere through the central service tube.

85
00:06:57,287 --> 00:07:01,194
The hoses and high voltage cable run through that atmospheric space.

86
00:07:01,194 --> 00:07:06,710
Chamber vacuum is outside the sealed stack, including the space beneath the outer ground shield.

87
00:07:06,710 --> 00:07:10,099
The outer shield tube is not the primary pressure boundary.

88
00:07:10,099 --> 00:07:15,500
The face seals through the copper, spacer, PTFE ring, and bottom body establish that boundary.

89
00:07:17,300 --> 00:07:20,898
At the front, the two inch target seats on the copper surface.

90
00:07:20,898 --> 00:07:25,598
The retaining ring overlaps its edge, and twelve front screws draw the ring down.

91
00:07:25,598 --> 00:07:31,343
The fitted titanium study target is two millimetres thick, matching the source clamp contact plane.

92
00:07:31,343 --> 00:07:34,940
That thickness is inferred, not a supplied target measurement.

93
00:07:34,940 --> 00:07:39,582
The ring keeps clearance from the outer copper face so it can apply target load.

94
00:07:39,582 --> 00:07:47,300
These front screws are independent of the rear cooling enclosure screws, allowing target changes without dismantling the water seals.

95
00:07:49,100 --> 00:07:52,429
The outer ground shield tube slides over the assembled head.

96
00:07:52,429 --> 00:07:55,148
Three radial screws fasten it to the bottom body.

97
00:07:55,148 --> 00:08:00,808
The upper dark space shield then locates inside the tube and is retained by six smaller radial screws.

98
00:08:00,808 --> 00:08:05,137
Its opening exposes the target region while covering the surrounding hardware.

99
00:08:05,137 --> 00:08:09,409
The source tube has clearance and slots rather than a precision sliding seal.

100
00:08:09,409 --> 00:08:17,233
This shield belongs to the grounded structure, and its fit must not be confused with the face seals that keep the dry interior at atmosphere.

101
00:08:19,033 --> 00:08:21,062
This completes the study assembly.

102
00:08:21,062 --> 00:08:31,620
It contains one hundred and twenty solid instances, including sixty five screws, twelve outer rods, two center discs, the five specified face O rings, and the reference KF seal.

103
00:08:31,620 --> 00:08:34,603
All solid bodies passed the native validity check.

104
00:08:34,603 --> 00:08:41,643
The documented interference regions are nominal screw shafts in thread sized bores, including the flagged yoke detail.

105
00:08:41,643 --> 00:08:44,267
The original machined geometry is preserved.

106
00:08:44,267 --> 00:08:55,900
Missing purchased hardware, sleeve dimensions, target thickness, and flexible routing remain labeled so this reconstruction can be studied without presenting estimates as original specifications.
